# tests/.skip-budget — how many tests are ALLOWED to skip, per suite.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   bats reports a `skip` as `ok`. A machine missing ddev, php or yq therefore
#   turns real assertions into green ticks and the suite still exits 0. That is
#   how `pl test --integration` came to print "Integration tests passed" over a
#   run in which 14 of 70 cases asserted nothing.
#
#   Declaring the number here makes the shortfall visible and, more importantly,
#   makes it MOVE ONLY IN ONE DIRECTION: if a run skips more than the budget the
#   suite fails; if it skips fewer, the runner tells you to lower the number in
#   the same MR. A skip that nobody had to think about is the failure mode.
#
# SHRINK-ONLY. Raising a number needs a reason in the commit message and is a
# statement that the machine got LESS able to verify, not that the code got
# better. Provision the missing tool on the runner first.
#
# Override per-run (CI, a deliberately reduced runner) with:
#   NWP_SKIP_BUDGET_UNIT / NWP_SKIP_BUDGET_INTEGRATION / NWP_SKIP_BUDGET_E2E
#
# Baseline captured 2026-07-26 on the dev workstation (bats 1.x, no ddev):
#   integration — 14 of 70 cases guard on `ENABLE_DDEV_TESTS=true`; they are
#                 the whole lifecycle half of the suite (install, backup/restore,
#                 copy, delete, deployment). CI runs with a budget of 0 against
#                 06-scripts-validation.bats only, which is why this number has
#                 never been enforced anywhere until now.
#   unit        — measured below; interpreter-dependent cases (php) are the bulk.

integration=14
unit=0
e2e=0
